/*******************************************************************************
	Jitting
*******************************************************************************/
The list is how the program is held, not how it runs. Interpreted: testOP walks
the list. Jitted: the jitter walks it once at compile time and emits straight-
line code; seq flattens, if becomes a branch, a loop a back-edge, break a jump.
First step, before any emitter: a recon of the new ops under jitter;, measured
against the rules for new rules. Today the new ops hand over to run time.
A split rule (decide below, build above) adds one label level; check it there.

/*******************************************************************************
	Open questions
*******************************************************************************/
1  A kant spelling for a rule action that rejects a match. Today only a null
   from a C++ action fails a match; no kant spelling produces one. SHORTCUt
   no longer needs it (SEQ 469 dropped its decision); the question stands for
   the next rule that must decide.
2  Two-part rule actions on one rule: a parse action (decides, may reject) and
   a runtime action (builds). One rule cannot carry ruleMethod= and code={}
   today. First read what parseAction / parseCondition already do.
   Read 2026-10-29: parseAction (Generate.rtn) runs a rule's C++ actor
   (builtinActoR) AS its parse, and a null from it fails the term -- a
   parse-time decide/reject slot, but C++ only and generated-parser only.
   parseCondition consumes nothing and matches on its min alone. Both carry
   "UNPINNED, no road reaches it": neither is tested, and neither lets a kant
   action reject (that is question 1). A row comes before anything leans on them.
3  Is a rule's decision always separable from its build? Watched through IF,
   the loops, break / continue, blocks and iterate (SEQ 473-486): none needed a
   parse decision -- each action only builds and refuses by name when a child
   hands up nothing. Still open in general (ELSEIf, action bodies).
4  Adjacent expressions -- SETTLED SEQ 468. EXPRESSIOn does not stop: like the
   old road, adjacent operands are ONE list value, but known by the tag
   ADJACENt (the old road's split is C++: interpretXPaccum's isLIST, SEQ 465).
   print qa qb:; prints 2 3 on both roads. An ADJACENt list meeting ANY
   operator (call and subscript included) refuses by name, "not built on the
   new road yet". What remains is question 5.
5  List-taking operators (Tony wants more than +%; which ones not yet named),
   PARKED with two shapes (SEQ 467): (A) one C++ helper isListArg (isLIST, or
   the tag ADJACENt) called by each of today's twelve list arms -- one call per
   new operator, but a kantGrammar tag spelled in C++; (B) EXPRESSIOn spreads
   the list at build, one instruct per member, for any op whose Operators
   entry carries takesList -- no C++, one attribute per new operator, but the
   value becomes the seq's, +%'s holder unwrap has no list to act on, and the
   spread sits in the slot code SEQ 448 R6 wants simplified. Tony, 2026-10-30:
   leave the REFUSED on an adjacent run in place (print items separate with ,).
6  Build order vs branch targets (2026-10-30). Children build first, so a
   break cannot name its loop -- the loop does not exist yet when the break is
   built. Only the loop could bind its breaks, by walking its own body at build
   (a build-time walk that NO HUNT would have to allow). Today the signal finds
   its landing at run time and nothing binds. Open until new-road loops jit,
   where a break wants a jump target.
/*******************************************************************************
	Standing rulings for the new grammar (refreshed 2026-10-29)
*******************************************************************************/
R0  New-grammar rules are spelled UPPERCASe: uppercase, last letter lowercase
    (EXPRESSIOn, STATEMENt, PRINt, If, Do).                     (SEQ 431)
R1  TOKENize, DEFINing and PRINTing stay as they are, shared with the old
    grammar; no new rule reuses their exact spellings.          (SEQ 431)
R2  Arguments with no operator are carried as a list first. No new link field
    (nextInParent) unless a list is shown not to do the job.    (SEQ 431)
R3  IF's shape: one instruct in its parent's seq. Its method runs the
    condition, runs one arm, and hands back the arm's result, or nothing when
    no arm ran.                                                 (SEQ 431, 473)
    Operand layout SETTLED (SEQ 472 R2): op ifThen, the argument a list
    [condition, then, else?]. The loops follow it.
R4  Expression tiers (tester's EXPRESSIOn, tier-split 920d1a81): arithmetic
    folds at once; compare, logic and assign each wait in their own slot; a
    looser operator closes every tighter slot first; the same tier closes its
    own, so a tier reads left to right; assign never closes assign -- a second
    assign nests, right to left. The end closes tightest first. testerChainT
    pins it.                                                    (SEQ 435 R2)
R5  Chained assign is accepted: a = b = c assigns right to left (TC-16).
                                                                (SEQ 435 R1)
R6  Bottom-up: each new rule is tested by calling it by name, rule("text").
    The search-list swap is dropped for now.                    (SEQ 438 R0)
R7  An action reads its component labels, builds an instruct in tester's
    layout (op [1], target [2], argument [3], method testOP) and returns it.
    Nothing runs inside an action.                              (SEQ 438 R1)
R8  New ops are C++ first, then kant. Each scaffold carries a dated TODO line,
    "retires when the kant action of the same name passes the same rows",
    retired by mapping, groups.ext line included. opSeq, opPrintList and
    opPrintShortcut are such scaffolds. shortcutTest was the new grammar's C++
    parse decider; since SEQ 469 only the old road calls it.   (SEQ 438 R4)
R9  Ruling A: a name the new road meets inside an action body that resolves
    to nothing becomes a local of that action, created when the action is
    built, once -- the set the old road creates (aCTionNamE) and the frame
    bracket saves. Beside the 2026-09-27 ruling (names become locals at
    compile time, once).                                        (SEQ 502 R0)
